WEBVTT

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:16.380
A signal is not a verdict. An employee account shows an unfamiliar
session, a new external forwarding rule, and an unusual download.
That is enough to investigate urgently, but not enough to name the
root cause.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:30.040
First, separate observations from explanations. Correlate identity
and application logs, check what the account can access, and
verify the activity with the employee through a trusted channel.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:48.140
MFA does not rule out token theft. An attacker may replay a stolen
session even after the real user completed multifactor
authentication. A legitimate VPN or approved export may also
explain individual signals, so test alternatives.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:01:09.680
Contain, scope, then verify. If unauthorized activity is
corroborated, restrict the affected access under your incident
process, revoke relevant sessions, and investigate forwarding
rules, consent grants, and new authentication methods or devices.
Preserve evidence while reducing ongoing harm.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:24.220
Do not promise that a password reset instantly ends every session.
Existing access tokens or application-issued sessions may remain
usable, depending on the application and its revocation support.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:43.880
Finish by checking that unauthorized access and persistence are
removed. State what data access is confirmed, what remains
unknown, and what evidence supports closure. In an interview,
explain the decision, the tradeoff, and the verification, not just
the tool you would open.
